      "properties": {
        "cnr_id": "comfyui-custom-scripts",
        "ver": "609f3afaa74b2f88ef9ce8d939626065e3247469",
        "Node name for S&R": "ShowText|pysssss"
      },
      "widgets_values": [
        "integrated_multimodal_description: [Shot 1] [0-4s] 1960s Shaw Brothers wuxia film style, saturated Technicolor, heavy film grain, studio bamboo forest set with painted backdrop and artificial mist. Two sword fighters clash in a tight bamboo grove — the cutting is a violent storm: a rapid montage of shots lasting half a second or less, the blade flashing faster than the eye can follow. A flurry of ultra-short shots: a hand gripping the sword hilt, eyes locking, feet pivoting on dirt, a blade slicing through bamboo leaves, sparks of metal-on-metal, a face jerking back. Each cut lands like a physical blow. The bamboo stalks whip through the foreground between cuts. The rhythm is percussive and relentless. [Shot 2] At 00:04.000 [4-8s] The montage climaxes — a rapid series of 6-frame shots of the final exchange, then one clean cut: one fighter's sword stops a millimeter from the other's throat. Sudden stillness after the storm, bamboo leaves slowly falling in the silence, mist drifting. The held breath of the fight.\n\noverall_soundscape: Metallic blade clashes in rapid succession, bamboo stalks snapping, cloth whipping, sharp exhales, the whistle of each swing, then a sudden dead silence broken by a single falling leaf.\n\nnon_diegetic_music: Machine-gun Peking opera drum and gong percussion matching every cut, accelerating to a peak, then cutting to total silence with one lingering gong resonance."
      ],
      "widgets_values_named": {
        "text_0": "integrated_multimodal_description: [Shot 1] [0-4s] 1960s Shaw Brothers wuxia film style, saturated Technicolor, heavy film grain, studio bamboo forest set with painted backdrop and artificial mist. Two sword fighters clash in a tight bamboo grove — the cutting is a violent storm: a rapid montage of shots lasting half a second or less, the blade flashing faster than the eye can follow. A flurry of ultra-short shots: a hand gripping the sword hilt, eyes locking, feet pivoting on dirt, a blade slicing through bamboo leaves, sparks of metal-on-metal, a face jerking back. Each cut lands like a physical blow. The bamboo stalks whip through the foreground between cuts. The rhythm is percussive and relentless. [Shot 2] At 00:04.000 [4-8s] The montage climaxes — a rapid series of 6-frame shots of the final exchange, then one clean cut: one fighter's sword stops a millimeter from the other's throat. Sudden stillness after the storm, bamboo leaves slowly falling in the silence, mist drifting. The held breath of the fight.\n\noverall_soundscape: Metallic blade clashes in rapid succession, bamboo stalks snapping, cloth whipping, sharp exhales, the whistle of each swing, then a sudden dead silence broken by a single falling leaf.\n\nnon_diegetic_music: Machine-gun Peking opera drum and gong percussion matching every cut, accelerating to a peak, then cutting to total silence with one lingering gong resonance."
      },
